      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "8479a10a-7f66-540d-8458-dda374fac829",
        ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2 t+1,\\qquad y=2 t^{3}+3 t,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=-2.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2 (-2)+1=-4+1=-3 \\\\ y & =2 (-2)^{3}+3 (-2)=-16-6=-22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(-3,-22).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2,\\qquad y'(t)=6 t^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}=\\frac{6 t^{2}+3}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{6 (-2)^{2}+3}{2}=\\frac{27}{2}=\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1)=(-3,-22)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{27}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny+22=\\frac{27}{2}(x+3).\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
