      "question": {
        ":question/id": "a69cf94a-f746-5a19-9f67-7603835405a5",
        ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4 t-1,\\qquad y=-2 t^{3}+5 t,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4 (1)-1=4-1=3 \\\\ y & =-2 (1)^{3}+5 (1)=-2+5=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(3,3).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=4,\\qquad y'(t)=-6 t^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}=\\frac{-6 t^{2}+5}{4}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{-6 (1)^{2}+5}{4}=\\frac{-1}{4}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1)=(3,3)$ and the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny-3=-\\frac{1}{4}(x-3).\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
