        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-2 (-2)+5=4+5=9 \\\\ y & =-2 (-2)^{3}+2 (-2)=16-4=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(9,12).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=-2,\\qquad y'(t)=-6 t^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}=\\frac{-6 t^{2}+2}{-2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{-6 (-2)^{2}+2}{-2}=\\frac{-22}{-2}=11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1)=(9,12)$ and the slope $m=11.$ We get\n\n$$\ny-12=11(x-9).\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
