        ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2 t+5,\\qquad y=t^{3}+5 t,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=-2.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2 (-2)+5=-4+5=1 \\\\ y & =(-2)^{3}+5 (-2)=-8-10=-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(1,-18).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2,\\qquad y'(t)=3 t^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}=\\frac{3 t^{2}+5}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{3 (-2)^{2}+5}{2}=\\frac{17}{2}=\\frac{17}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1)=(1,-18)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{17}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny+18=\\frac{17}{2}(x-1).\n$$",
